Sculpt the **Aegis**, a colossal hard-edged six-legged walking fortress,
as a static 3D mesh built with the Dual Contouring tool (`dc`), 120x110x150, using
only that tool.

## How sculpting works

The only way to shape the surface is the `dc` binary already on your `PATH`. It is
the sole channel: rather than placing discrete voxels, you composite a **continuous
signed-distance field** by calling `dc` **one operation at a time** — unioning
material in with `add-box` / `add-sphere` / `add-ellipsoid` / `add-cylinder` and
carving it away with the matching `subtract-*` primitives, each in an opaque
`#rrggbb` color. `dc` is the **high-fidelity, sharp-feature-preserving** extractor:
it keeps crisp edges and corners instead of rounding them, and it exposes a
`--sharp` / `--smooth` tag on primitives (the low-poly and smooth extractors do
not). Build through the tool — the surface it extracts is what is scored.

The field starts **empty**. Colors are **opaque** `#rrggbb` — there is no
transparency. After each call the binary re-composites the field, extracts the mesh
to `mesh.glb`, and re-renders a 3D preview to `model.png`. The recorded operations
are the authoritative output that is scored. A blank starting preview and empty
field are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
dc add-box --x 60 --y 55 --z 74 --width 76 --height 22 --depth 96 --color "#c69a4b" --sharp
```

Run `dc --help` to list every operation and `dc <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`model.png` after your calls** to see your progress and decide what to do next.

